@article{ WOS:001688684600001,
Author = {Naili, Chloe and Bally, Etienne and Cholat, Florent and Agarande, Aliya
   and Arbus, Christophe and Attou, Anya and Goze, Tudi},
Title = {Musical practice as embodied cognitive remediation in schizophrenia: Two
   narrative reviews},
Journal = {ANNALES MEDICO-PSYCHOLOGIQUES},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {184},
Number = {2},
Pages = {121-136},
Month = {FEB},
Abstract = {Context. - Schizophrenia is a chronic and disabling psychiatric disorder
   that affects about 1\% of the population. It is characterized by a
   complex combination of positive, negative, disorganized, and cognitive
   symptoms. Cognitive impairments-especially in memory, attention,
   processing speed, and executive functions-are among the strongest
   predictors of psychosocial functioning. Social cognition is also
   frequently impaired and impacts emotional recognition, theory of mind,
   and interpersonal adjustment. As pharmacological treatments remain
   ineffective for cognitive symptoms, cognitive remediation is now
   considered the gold standard. This narrative review explores the
   potential of music practice as a non-pharmacological tool for cognitive
   remediation in schizophrenia. Music engages multiple cognitive and
   emotional processes, and has demonstrated positive effects both in
   healthy individuals and in clinical populations, including patients with
   stroke and Alzheimer's disease. These effects may stem from the
   multimodal and embodied nature of music practice, which involves motor
   coordination, memory, symbolic processing, and emotional engagement. To
   deepen the theoretical framework, this article draws on embodiment-a
   cognitive science approach that considers the body as central to
   cognition, structured around the 4E model: Embodied, Extended, Embedded,
   and Enactive. This framework supports a grounded exploration of
   music-based interventions in schizophrenia. Method. - This article is
   based on two narrative reviews conducted using PubMed: one on the
   relationship between embodiment and schizophrenia (RN1), and the other
   on embodiment and music (RN2). Each review followed a two-step selection
   process to exclude duplicates and irrelevant articles, focusing only on
   those addressing both embodiment and either schizophrenia or music. The
   selected articles were organized according to the 4E framework:
   Embodied, Extended, Embedded, and Enactive cognition. A cross-analysis
   of both corpora revealed recurrent embodied processes-temporal
   perception, interactional synchrony, and social cognition-justifying a
   dedicated subchapter. Although the music review included both listening
   and practice, the final focus was placed on active music-making, which
   more directly engages the embodied, extended, and enactive dimensions of
   cognition, and aligns with the principles of cognitive remediation.
   Results. - The first narrative review (RN1) analyzed 23 articles
   exploring schizophrenia through the lens of embodiment. Cognitive
   impairments in schizophrenia were reframed as disruptions in the
   sensorimotor integration between body, action, and environment. Studies
   highlighted altered posture, impaired source memory, and disconnection
   from bodily experience as central to cognitive and social dysfunction.
   Embodied therapies, such as Body-Oriented Psychotherapy, showed clinical
   benefits by reengaging patients through bodily experience. While
   evidence was limited for the ``Extended{''} dimension, the
   ``Embedded{''} and ``Enactive{''} aspects revealed impairments in
   temporal perception, affordance processing, and interactive synchrony.
   Overall, embodiment offers a valuable framework for understanding and
   addressing cognitive and relational deficits in schizophrenia. The
   second narrative review (RN2) included 27 articles exploring the
   relationship between music and embodiment. Findings emphasized that
   musical cognition is not confined to the brain but involves the whole
   body, engaging sensorimotor, emotional, and environmental processes.
   Music practice illustrated all four dimensions of the 4E framework. As
   an embodied activity, it integrates bodily gestures, memory, and
   emotion. As extended, instruments and notations shape and extend
   cognitive processes. As embedded, music-making is socially and
   materially situated. Finally, music is enactive, involving real-time
   interaction with sound and others. These embodied dynamics, especially
   rhythmic coordination, support social bonding, emotional regulation, and
   cognitive integration, making music a promising tool for therapeutic
   engagement. Discussion. - Although these narrative reviews are
   non-systematic and rely on limited empirical samples, they highlight
   meaningful theoretical and clinical insights. Applying the 4E framework
   of embodiment (Embodied, Extended, Embedded, Enactive) revealed key
   parallels between the cognitive processes involved in music practice and
   those disrupted in schizophrenia. Music engages bodily dynamics
   essential for attention, memory, and executive functions, while
   schizophrenia is marked by sensorimotor disconnection and impaired
   social engagement. Three embodied dimensions-temporal perception,
   interactional synchrony, and social cognition-emerged as particularly
   relevant. These processes, often altered in schizophrenia, are actively
   mobilized through rhythmic coordination, group improvisation, and
   musical interaction. Music thus appears as a promising medium for
   relational and emotional remediation. Structured musical activities may
   offer more targeted cognitive benefits than therapeutic approaches
   alone. Moreover, group-based practice could enhance social cognition by
   fostering synchrony and embodied co-regulation. These findings encourage
   further exploration of music as an embodied tool for cognitive
   remediation in schizophrenia. Conclusion. - Despite its conceptual
   heterogeneity, embodiment offers a complementary perspective to
   traditional cognitive and neurobiological models of schizophrenia. This
   framework highlights the central role of the body in cognition and
   reveals overlaps between musical engagement and cognitive disruptions in
   schizophrenia. Music practice may support embodied cognitive processes
   and act as a relevant tool for cognitive remediation. A structured,
   pedagogical, and group-based format could enhance both cognitive and
   social functions, highlighting the need for further research comparing
   pedagogical and therapeutic frameworks. (c) 2025 The Authors. Published
   by Elsevier Masson SAS. This is an open access article under the CC BY
   license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).},
